//? Title: Fletcher-16: two residues, exact error patterns and real aliases
//? Subtitle: Actual byte folds, the original examples, streaming composition and complete native corruption scans
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A receiver can compare a short tag with a recomputed checksum to detect some accidental
changes to a byte stream. The important question is which changes are detected, not
whether the tag looks complicated. Raw Fletcher-16 maintains two sums modulo 255,
which detects every single-input-bit change when the original tag is held fixed.
It also has exact aliases, including a byte changing between zero and 255 and certain
transpositions. This report preserves the original examples, inspects actual native
states, counts complete corruption candidates, and distinguishes those guarantees
from probabilistic claims or protocol-specific check-byte formats.
)

\toc

> The method and its raw-tag convention

Start with A=B=0. For every unsigned input byte x, perform

$$
A\leftarrow(A+x)\bmod255,\qquad
B\leftarrow(B+A)\bmod255,\qquad
\operatorname{tag}=(B\ll8)\mathbin{|}A.
$$
\alttext(The first residue adds the byte modulo two hundred and fifty-five. The second residue adds the newly updated first residue using the same modulus. The raw tag places B in its high byte and A in its low byte.)

The library uses canonical residues 0 through 254, so both an octet zero and an octet 255
contribute residue zero. Empty input has tag zero; embedded zero bytes do not terminate
the input. This is the original library's raw packed tag. Protocols can instead compute
complemented check bytes at prescribed header offsets and reserve special values.
Those wire conventions are not supplied by this API.

Each byte costs two modular recurrence updates. The implementation reduces after every
byte, not in delayed batches. The residues fit in eight bits, while transient additions
need nine; the core uses sixteen-bit arithmetic. There is O(n) work and constant ordinary
state, without a hardware speed ranking against Adler-32, CRC or plain sums.

> Two bytes: inspect the actual fold

For bytes [1,2], after the first byte A=B=1. After the second, A=3 and B=4, giving raw
tag 0x0403. Equivalently, A=1+2 and B=2×1+1×2. The program reads these states from
the native byte kernel; it does not implement a second checksum in the report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fletcher_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <limits>
#include <print>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
const auto t=dm::algorithms::fletcher16_trace(std::string("\1\2",2));
  std::print("| input position | byte | A before | B before | A after | B after |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto&s:t.steps)std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",s.index,static_cast<unsigned>(s.byte),s.before_a,s.before_b,s.after_a,s.after_b);
  std::print("\nActual final residues A {}; B {}; raw packed tag 0x{:04X}.\n",t.sum1,t.sum2,t.checksum);
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(60,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle b;b.width=3.5;b.height=1.9;b.font_size=24;
  g.node("start",{0,0},"Initial state\nA = 0\nB = 0",b).node("first",{5.4,0},std::format("Read byte {}\nA = {}\nB = {}",static_cast<unsigned>(t.steps[0].byte),t.steps[0].after_a,t.steps[0].after_b),b).node("second",{10.8,0},std::format("Read byte {}\nA = {}\nB = {}",static_cast<unsigned>(t.steps[1].byte),t.steps[1].after_a,t.steps[1].after_b),b).edge("start","first","A, then B").edge("first","second","A, then B").text({5.4,-2.5},"The second sum uses the newly updated first sum",25).text({5.4,-4},"A = 1 + 2 = 3; B = 2 × 1 + 2 = 4",25).save_svg("build/fletcher_tiny.svg");
  if(t.checksum!=0x0403)throw std::runtime_error("tiny Fletcher identity");
}
