> What the examples establish

The original reference, all 88 one-bit checks and the detected ABCD/ABDC swap are retained.
The raw recurrence also proves a general single-input-bit guarantee, while complete byte
replacement and explicit transposition controls expose its exact exceptions. A plain sum
misses the original reordering but catches the zero-to-255 substitution that Fletcher
misses, so a universal strength ladder would be misleading. Corruption models, tag width,
message structure and implementation costs must be specified before comparing algorithms.
These are accidental-error checksums, not authentication tags. No external dataset is
needed for the finite byte domains and controlled error patterns studied here.

> References

- J. G. Fletcher (1982), [An Arithmetic Checksum for Serial Transmissions](https://doi.org/10.1109/TCOM.1982.1095369), IEEE Transactions on Communications 30(1),247–252. Citation verified; the modular recurrence and protocol distinctions here are supported by the accessible RFC sources below.
- [RFC 926](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc926.html), appendices A.3 and C.2–C.4: modulo-255 arithmetic, zero/255 aliases, single-bit detection and protocol check-byte construction. Its protocol reserves an all-zero check field; the library's raw zero tag is a valid arithmetic result.
- J. Zweig and C. Partridge (1990), [RFC 1146](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1146.html), appendix I: two zero-initialised one's-complement accumulators and the weighted second sum. The raw packed integer convention in this report is specified independently of any wire representation.

